/**
\addtogroup Physics

\ref pdg::PhysicsBody controls motion, forces and material properties;
\ref pdg::Collider supplies collision geometry and contact events, and
\ref pdg::PhysicsConstraint connects bodies. Sprites and Parts share these APIs.

\note Animation physics requires Chipmunk in C++ and JavaScript. The Basic
solver supports simple shared PhysicsBody/PhysicsConstraint use; it is not an
animation-rig backend and will not be expanded to support complex animation
physics. Use supportsAnimationPhysics() to check build capability and enable
Chipmunk on the SpriteLayer before setupAnimationPhysics() or
setupPhysicsFromAnimationRig(). Ordinary authored playback and pose IK do not
require physics.

\section physics_solver_comparison Basic and Chipmunk solver comparison

Compare the native solvers using the same physical inputs. Step time measures CPU
cost; overlap, joint error and settling describe the resulting physical behavior.
The measurements below apply to the native engine used by both language APIs;
they do not measure JavaScript binding or callback overhead.

\snippet{doc} CONTACT_REUSE_RESULTS.md physics-solver-results

\section physics_solver_viewer Watch the solvers side by side

This viewer replays recorded runs of the actual C++ solvers. Each run simulates
2,000 dynamic bodies; a representative sample is displayed so individual contacts
and joints remain visible. Both views use the same scale and simulation time.
Playback speed is independent of the CPU timings above. No server, engine build
or internet connection is needed after downloading the documentation.

Select approaching circles, touching circles with opposing forces, box stacks,
joint chains or box piles. Switch between Sprite and Part owners, enable Chipmunk
sleeping for the box and chain scenes, or pause, scrub, step and slow playback.
In the circle grids, rows vary offset or force direction and columns increase
speed or force. Yellow arrows disappear when the touching pair's forces stop.

